im/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:892:refinement adds no core value and keeps the pressure of `indeterminate`,\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:921:Out of frame or indeterminate. The boundary between these values is drawn\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:936:- `indeterminate` when the available admissible evidence cannot decide the\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:940:  `out_of_frame` and `indeterminate` each have a lawful ground; the result\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:953:  `incomplete` under\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:991:### Foundation to law: binding a project to the method\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1027:### Foundation to law\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1062:| P6 (independent) | By P6 meaning, evidence, application, operation and decision are separate authorities, so semantic law defines what the evaluation means, evidence supplies observations, the evaluator applies the declared relation, the existing operation owner supplies any operation grant, and decision authority disposes the result; a frame declaration keeps these roles distinguishable even when one actor performs several. The evaluator applies the frame's declared invariants, evidence rules and result algebra and records every assumption whose failure could change the result | [RF-007](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#rf-007-semantic-evidence-and-verdict-separation); [ACTIVATION §2](../ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope) Evaluation, outside this member | none; [EVALUATION §2](../EVALUATION.md#2-separation) applies this law to review (family F6) | 7, 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1064:| P0 (recovered), P5 (derived from P0 and P6, with P0's open-world premise), P6 (independent); instance premise a | By P0 an actor's envelope is finite, and by P5 a step cannot report success on what it did not hold, so frame adequacy includes actor usability. When the material manifold exceeds an actor's envelope, the lawful responses are another actor, a refined frame or outer contract, sequenced activations, a lawful compression or `out_of_frame`; silently omitting material relations to fit is never one of them, and a frame never silently grows beyond the selected actor's envelope. Actor capability may justify a different frame size or activation sequence, and can neither waive an invariant nor widen authority | [RF-014](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#rf-014-capability-fit); [ACTIVATION §2](../ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope) step 3, the responses when the context does not fit, outside this member; [FOUNDATIONS §2 Frame objects](../FOUNDATIONS.md#frame-objects), Capability envelope, outside this member; [§11](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#11-the-activation-packet), frame size; [§12](../REFERENCE_FRAME.md#12-violations) item 10 | the selection among these responses: human authority, directly or in advance through the grant; which responses a step selects within its grant (refinement, sequencing, compression) and which re-enter the owner of the grant or envelope (another actor, a larger envelope): ACTIVATION §2 step 3 | 7, 8 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1066:| P0 (recovered, with its actor-change premise), P3 (independent), P6 (independent); instance premise e | By P0's actor-change premise and P3 an input is acquired by exact identity from the selected basis, and by P6 absence from view grants nothing, so declared inputs are acquired by exact identity, and only from the exact selected basis: a declared input absent from the actor's view is neither licence to read elsewhere nor evidence that it is absent, and a candidate source, a moving selector or another basis is no substitute. Where a derived surface is stale, missing or insufficient for a named question, the deciding owner sections are acquired directly. Acquisition is read-only, grants no role, operation or decision, and a missing binding is returned with its outcome before any dependent action | [ACTIVATION §2](../ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope) step 4 and Acquisition, outside this member | acquiring a current derived surface in preference to its owning sections where it serves the task: ACTIVATION §2 Acquisition; which derived surfaces are published: their publisher, and none owns a determination | 2, 7 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/frames/bootstrap.md:1087:### Foundation to law: chain, outcome and build-tenant binding\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/ACTIVATION.md:1:# ACTIVATION\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/ACTIVATION.md:27:## 1. The step\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/ACTIVATION.md:72:| P2 (independent), P3 (independent), P1 (independent), P6 (independent); instance premise a | By P2 the destination is a set of required conditions that several realised states may satisfy, by P3 the current state is the bound subject with its observations and dependencies, by P1 the required conditions, the observations and any predictive model are different accounts, and by P6 lawfulness confers no delivery or acceptance, so construction binds A and Z explicitly, works under the declared calculi, and keeps the three accounts apart | [Construction relation](#construction-relation), Binding A and Z, Three accounts | the governing specification that supplies Z: the owning authority of the selected outcome; the declared calculi: SPEC_METHOD §11 for a build tenant | 1, 5, 6, 8 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/ACTIVATION.md:75:| P1 (independent), P2 (independent), P6 (independent), P5 (derived from P0 and P6, with P0's open-world premise); instance premise a | By P1 a discrepancy between an observation and the required conditions has candidate causes of different kinds that are not conflated, of which the relation names four, by P2 the response serves the destination, by P6 each repair goes to the owner of its cause, and by P5 an undecided cause stays undecided, so feedback compares observations with the governing conditions, diagnoses among the four causes, preserves unresolved alternatives and routes each supported response to its owner; a bug is one intake category of change work, which SPEC_METHOD §4 applies at intake | [Construction relation](#construction-relation), Feedback and diagnosis | severity and priority policies: the adopting Product's work carriers ([TICKETS](TICKETS.md)) | 5, 9 |\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/ACTIVATION.md:111:- Row 14, preservation, progress and completion, and evaluation and completion: [EVALUATION §1](EVALUATION.md#1-claims); [§3](#3-step-results-and-disposition); [§6](#6-cost-and-proportionality); [FOUNDATIONS §4 Assertions and observation](FOUNDATIONS.md#assertions-and-observation).\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/ACTIVATION.md:116:### Transitions\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/ACTIVATION.md:149:Discovery. Construction, observation and evaluation change the current\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.1/standards/ACTIVATION.md:150:state and what is known about it. A new observation may add obligations,\n/Users/jim/Library/Application Support/STDO/releases/v3.0.1-rc.